evidence[6]:
  - Loaded current Kei index/requirements analyst role, specification convention/workflow and selected rules; external project resolved to assigned root and project AGENTS loaded.
  - Source discovery stayed at baseline files for lifecycle commands, inventory, ownership, installers, bootstrap, relevant offline tests and dependent current docs. No dev/.env or secret reads.
  - Draft distinguishes F-001 through F-011 source facts, D-* human decisions, C-* inherited constraints and A-* approval-stage assumptions/proposals.
  - Structural check found 22 unique sequential REQ IDs, 21 AC IDs, 9 UC IDs and 48 valid local links; absent dev/AGENTS.md and dev/docs/AGENTS.md confirmed.
  - Final unsafe/incomplete uninstall choice incorporated as D-011 and closed Q-001. Record is retained until repair rather than silently detached.
  - Runtime tests were not run because authored changes are documentation only. No implementation or host migration acceptance is claimed.
findings[4]:
  - Current install replaces expected-tools, so install flyline creates a source-derived overwrite risk; actual host file content was not inspected or reported as observed.
  - Current healthy inventory omits broken links/payloads and cannot alone initialize the required maintenance record.
  - Existing ownership validator requires current definition and payload. Specification requires conservative retired/missing behavior without choosing replacement state schema or weakening deletion proof.
  - Current fzf instructions in docs/install.md and dev/docs/fzf-retirement-plan.md require later migration to the new CLI and automatic state while preserving phase-1 evidence and phase-2 host gate.
assumptions[3]:
  - A-001 keeps current profile composition rather than redesigning package selection.
  - A-002 identifies proposed lexical CLI, status and exit details for approval with the concrete draft.
  - A-003 leaves internal low-level installer responsibility to later planning without a parallel user lifecycle.
